Points $D,E,F$ lie on sides $BC,CA,AB$, respectively, of an equilateral triangle $ABC$ of side length $3$. They satisfy $BD=1$ and $\angle ADE=\angle DEF=60^\circ$. Find the length of segment $AF$.

Here $XY$ denotes the length of segment $XY$.

[asy]
import geometry;
settings.tex="none";
defaultpen(black+linewidth(0.65));
real cross2(pair u,pair v) { return u.x*v.y-u.y*v.x; }
pair meet(pair p,pair v,pair q,pair w) { return p+cross2(q-p,w)/cross2(v,w)*v; }
size(245);
pair B=(0,0),C=(3,0),A=(1.5,1.5*sqrt(3)),D=(1,0);
pair E=meet(D,rotate(-60)*(A-D),A,C-A);
pair F=meet(E,rotate(-60)*(D-E),A,B-A);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D--E--F);
label("A",A,N);label("B",B,SW);label("C",C,SE);
label("D",D,S);label("E",E,(1,0));label("F",F,W);
[/asy]

A regular hexagon is inscribed in a rectangle as shown in the figure. If the areas of the shaded triangle and shaded quadrilateral are $20$ and $23$, respectively, find the area of the regular hexagon.

[asy]
import geometry;
settings.tex="none";
defaultpen(black+linewidth(0.65));
size(235);
pair[] V;
for(int i=0;i<6;++i) V.push(dir(100-60*i));
real xmin=V[5].x,xmax=V[2].x,ymin=V[3].y,ymax=V[0].y;
pair NW=(xmin,ymax),SW=(xmin,ymin),NE=(xmax,ymax),SE=(xmax,ymin);
fill(NW--V[0]--V[5]--cycle,gray(0.84));
fill(V[5]--V[4]--V[3]--SW--cycle,gray(0.84));
draw(NW--NE--SE--SW--cycle);
path p=V[0];for(int i=1;i<6;++i) p=p--V[i];draw(p--cycle);
label("20",(NW+V[0]+V[5])/3);
label("23",(xmin+0.16,ymin+0.15));
[/asy]

There is a figure consisting of $15$ circles and $20$ line segments as shown. Write one of $0,1,2$ in each circle. Define the beauty of a filling to be the number of the $20$ line segments whose two endpoint circles contain numbers differing by $1$. Let $M$ be the greatest possible beauty. How many fillings have beauty $M$?

Fillings that coincide after rotation or reflection are counted as different.

[asy]
import geometry;
settings.tex="none";
defaultpen(black+linewidth(0.65));
size(255);
pair[] O,M,I;
for(int k=0;k<5;++k) O.push(3*dir(90+72*k));
for(int k=0;k<5;++k) { M.push((O[k]+O[(k+1)%5])/2);I.push(1.15*dir(126+72*k)); }
for(int k=0;k<5;++k) {
  draw(O[k]--M[k]--O[(k+1)%5]);
  draw(M[k]--I[k]);draw(I[k]--I[(k+1)%5]);
}
for(int k=0;k<5;++k) for(pair p:new pair[]{O[k],M[k],I[k]}) filldraw(circle(p,0.28),white,black+linewidth(0.5));
[/asy]

Place some tiles consisting of four cells as shown in the figure on a $5\times5$ grid, along the grid lines. Tiles may overlap, but no tile may protrude beyond the grid. If each cell is covered by at least $0$ and at most $2$ tiles, find the greatest possible number of cells covered by at least one tile.

The tiles may be rotated or reflected.

[asy]
import geometry;
settings.tex="none";
defaultpen(black+linewidth(0.65));
size(175);
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,1)--(3,1)--(3,2)--(1,2)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle,black+linewidth(1.4));
draw((1,0)--(1,1),dotted);draw((1,1)--(2,1),dotted);draw((2,1)--(2,2),dotted);
[/asy]
